  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "With n = 2k + 1 you get n² − 1 = 4k(k + 1), so you still need k(k + 1) to be even.",
    "Instead, split the odd numbers by their remainder mod 4: n = 4k + 1 or n = 4k + 3."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Case $n = 4k + 1$: $n^2 - 1 = 16k^2 + 8k = 8(2k^2 + k)$.",
    "Case $n = 4k + 3$: $n^2 - 1 = 16k^2 + 24k + 8 = 8(2k^2 + 3k + 1)$.",
    "Every odd integer is of one of these forms, so $8 \\mid (n^2 - 1)$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "hammack-bop3",
    "section": "Chapter 5 exercises, #17 (p. 136)",
    "note": "This exact exercise, restated in ProofLab's words. Uses Definition 4.4 and proof by cases."
  },
